{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"bpf1lbvh6","tool_use_id":"toolu_0176ggzrz4R5SD8hEQvQ2XyX","status":"completed","output_file":"/tmp/claude-441489/-scratch-users-kushinm-auto-psych-outer-loop-live-runs-run3-repo/a0b3f086-270b-4edd-a254-abbbcabae6fb/tasks/bpf1lbvh6.output","summary":"Background command \"cd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop; D=iter_0/candidate_0\ncat > $D/hypothesis.md << 'EOF'\nRefinement of the incumbent `length_normalised_chance_vs_motif`: people still judge a sequence random to the extent their own model of a fair coin explains it better than the regular generators (a switch-biased Markov coin, a biased coin, and a repeating short motif with occasional slips), with the generators' unknowns averaged out and the evidence weighed per flip. The single change is that the one distortion of the normative account — the believed switch rate of a fair coin — is held differently by each person, drawn from a population: most people believe chance over-alternates, but some believe chance is streaky, so they credit sequences with fewer switches as more random, addressing the critique that a sizeable minority of participants systematically prefer the sequence with fewer switches.\nEOF\necho personal_chance_switch_belief > $D/model_name.txt\npython3 - << 'EOF'\nsrc=open('models/length_normalised_chance_vs_motif.py').read()\nsrc=src.replace('''    # Believed switch probability of a fair coin (logit scale; 0 = normative).\n    logit_q = pm.Normal(\"logit_q\", mu=0.0, sigma=1.0)\n    q = pm.Deterministic(\"q\", pm.math.sigmoid(logit_q))\n''','''    # Person-specific believed switch probability of a fair coin (logit scale;\n    # 0 = normative, > 0 over-alternating, < 0 streaky), non-centred population.\n    mu_logit_q = pm.Normal(\"mu_logit_q\", mu=0.0, sigma=1.0)\n    sigma_logit_q = pm.HalfNormal(\"sigma_logit_q\", sigma=0.5)\n    z_q = pm.Normal(\"z_q\", mu=0.0, sigma=1.0, shape=400)\n    logit_q = mu_logit_q + sigma_logit_q * z_q\n''')\nsrc=src.replace('score_diff = (switch_diff * logit_q - log_regular_diff)','score_diff = (switch_diff * logit_q[participant_id] - log_regular_diff)')\ndoc_old=src.split('\"\"\"')[1]\nsrc=src.replace(doc_old,'''Person-specific belief about how often a fair coin switches.\n\nRefinement of `length_normalised_chance_vs_motif`. A sequence looks random to\nthe extent the person's model of a fair coin (switching with probability q)\nexplains it better than the regular generators (Markov coin, biased coin,\nrepeating motif with slips; unknowns integrated out), the log evidence\ndifference weighed per flip by a fitted power of length, with person-specific\ndecision sensitivity. Single change: q -- the one distortion of the normative\nchance model -- is person-specific, drawn from a population on the logit\nscale, so some people may believe chance is streaky (q < 1/2) and prefer\nsequences with fewer switches while most believe it over-alternates.\n''',1)\nopen('iter_0/candidate_0/candidate.py','w').write(src)\nEOF\ncd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/runs/run3/repo && /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python -m src.pipelines.inner_loop.check_candidate --candidate-dir /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/iter_0/candidate_0 --responses /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/responses.csv 2>&1 | tail -5\" completed (exit code 0)","uuid":"3c4d17fd-8170-48ec-b34f-8ff207f0cd26","session_id":"a0b3f086-270b-4edd-a254-abbbcabae6fb"}
